<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wilhelm II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">abdicated <a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wilhelm II was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, reigning from 1888 until his abdication in 1918. After dismissing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-battleship">Bismarck</a> he pursued an assertive world policy, including a large naval programme that alienated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. His dismissal of Bismarck in 1890 exposed the system's fundamental weakness, and he abdicated on 9 November 1918 as revolution and defeat brought the empire to an end.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii.jpg" alt="Wilhelm II" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Studio of Thomas Heinrich Voigt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiser_Wilhelm_II_of_Germany_-_1902_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wilhelm was born on 27 January 1859 at the Crown Prince's Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, the son of Prince Frederick William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and Victoria, Princess Royal, the eldest child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>. He was the eldest of Victoria's 42 grandchildren. After 1871 he was second in line to the imperial throne, which under the constitution was held by the Prussian king.</p><p>His birth was traumatic, since the infant was in the breech position. His father succeeded as Frederick III after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> on 9 March 1888, but was already suffering from incurable throat cancer and died after 99 days, and on 15 June 1888 the 29-year-old Wilhelm became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bismarck's successor as chancellor, Leo von Caprivi, was followed by Chlodwig von Hohenlohe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schillingsfurst">Schillingsfürst</a> in 1894 and by Bernhard von Bülow in 1900, whom Wilhelm regarded as his own Bismarck. Wilhelm promoted the arts and sciences and sponsored the Kaiser Wilhelm Society for scientific research. His impatient, impulsive handling of foreign policy brought a series of incidents: the Kruger telegram of 1896, his speech of 1900 telling German soldiers leaving for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to be merciless in the spirit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, and a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> on 31 March 1905 that set off the Moroccan Crisis.</p><p>The Daily Telegraph Affair of 1908 damaged him badly, and he had less influence for the rest of his reign. A naval programme under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-von-tirpitz">Alfred von Tirpitz</a>, from 1897, set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><p>In the war he was a shadow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a>, confined to ceremonial duties; by 1916 the empire was effectively a military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> under Hindenburg and Ludendorff. He could not decide whether to abdicate in the revolution of 1918, hoping to keep the Prussian crown, but the imperial crown was tied to it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wilhelm went into exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and lived at Doorn. In 1922 he published the first volume of his memoirs, insisting that he was not guilty of starting the war. His first wife, Augusta Victoria, died on 11 April 1921, and on 5 November 1922 he married Hermine Reuss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greiz">Greiz</a>. He died of a pulmonary embolism at Doorn on 4 June 1941, aged 82.</p><p>Hitler wanted a state funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, but Wilhelm had ordered that his body not return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> unless the monarchy was restored. Writing about him has followed three trends: court writers who saw a martyr, those who judged him unable to bear his responsibilities, and, after 1950, scholars seeking an objective portrait.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wilhelm II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The German revolution, begun by the sailors' mutiny at Kiel, reached Berlin on 9 November 1918, when Max von Baden announced the abdication of <b>Wilhelm II</b> as Emperor and King of Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Peters, named Reichskommissar for the Kilimanjaro Region in 1891 under Soden, was recalled in 1892 and in 1897, after three investigations, deprived of his commission, his title and his pension; Kaiser <b>Wilhelm II</b> nevertheless gave him the title of 'retired Reichskommissar' in 1905.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Wilhelm II</b>'s dismissal of Bismarck in 1890 exposed the system's weakness.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The country stayed neutral in the First World War, mobilising its army throughout and later granting asylum to the deposed <b>Kaiser Wilhelm II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The conflict between Kruger’s government, the mining magnates, Cecil Rhodes and the British High Commissioner Alfred Milner drew the great powers into the region, and the telegram of congratulation that <b>Kaiser Wilhelm II</b> sent Kruger after the Jameson Raid strained Anglo-German relations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On 9 November 1918 Philipp Scheidemann of the Majority Social Democrats proclaimed the German Republic at the Reichstag building, the abdication of Kaiser <b>Wilhelm II</b> was announced, and Prince Max of Baden handed the chancellorship to Friedrich Ebert.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kronprinzenpalais, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Huis Doorn, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.4&amp;year=1918" aria-label="Kronprinzenpalais on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Wilhelm II"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5691 5568h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5691 5568h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.4&amp;year=1918">What was here in 1918? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
